'''unapplausive''' (un-a̤-plâ′siv), ''a.'' Not applauding; not cheering or encouraging by or as by applause.
{{smaller block|Instead of getting a soft fence against the cold, shadowy, ''unapplausive'' audience of his life, had he only given it a more substantial presence?
{{right|''George Eliot'', Middlemarch, xx. (''Davies.'')}}}}

'''unappliable''' (un-a̤-plī′a̤-bl), ''a.'' Inapplicable. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Best books to a naughty mind are not ''unappliable'' to occasions of evill. {{right|''Milton'', Areopagitica, p. 16.}}}}

'''unapplicable''' (un-ap′li-ka̤-bl), ''a.'' Inapplicable. ''Boyle'', Works, II. 485. [Rare.]

'''unapplied''' (un-a̤-plīd′), ''a.'' Not specially applied; not put or directed to some special object or purpose.
{{smaller block|Men dedicated to a private, free, ''unapplied'' course of life. {{right|''Bacon'', Advancement of Learning, ii.}}}}

'''unappreciable''' (un-a̤-prē′shi-a̤-bl), ''a.'' Inappreciable. [Rare.]

'''unappreciated''' (un-a̤-prē′shi-ā-ted), ''a.'' Not appreciated. {{smaller|(''a'') Not perceived or detected. (''b'') Not estimated at the true worth; not sufficiently valued.}}

'''unappreciative''' (un-a̤-prē′shi-ā-tiv), ''a.'' Inappreciative. ''The Academy'', May 3, 1890, p. 309.

'''unapprehended''' (un-ap-rẹ̄-hen′ded), ''a.'' '''1.''' Not apprehended; not taken.—'''2.''' Not understood, perceived, or conceived of.
{{smaller block|They of whom God is altogether ''unapprehended'' are but few in number. {{right|''Hooker'', Eccles. Polity, v. 2.}}}}

'''unapprehensible''' (un-ap-rē-hen′si-bl), ''a.'' Inapprehensible. ''South'', Sermons, V. v.

