¶
'''unapt''' (un-apt′), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unapt''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''apt''.] '''1.''' Not apt; not ready or inclined.
{{smaller block|<poem>I am a soldier, and ''unapt'' to weep.</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., v. 3. 133.}}
A most merciful man, as ready to forgive as ''unapt'' to take or give an offence.
{{right|''Penn'', Rise and Progress of Quakers, v.}}}}
'''2.''' Dull; not ready to learn: same as ''inapt'', 2.
{{smaller block|Very dull and ''unapt''. {{right|''Bacon.''}}}}
'''3.''' Unfit; inappropriate; unsuitable; not qualified; not disposed.
{{smaller block|<poem>Was never man or woman yet bigete
That was ''unapt'' to soferen loves hete
Celestial, or elles love of kynde.</poem>
{{right|''Chaucer'', Troilus, i. 978.}}
''Unapt'' I am, not only because of painful study, but also for this short warning.
{{right|''Latimer'', 1st Sermon bef. Edw. VI., 1549.}}
<poem>Why are our bodies soft and weak and smooth,
''Unapt'' to toil and trouble in the world?</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., v. 2. 166.}}
No ''unapt'' type of the sluggish and wavering movement of that mind. {{right|''Macaulay'', Hist. Eng., xvii.}}}}

¶
'''unaptly''' (un-apt′li), ''adv.'' Unfitly; improperly.

'''unaptness''' (un-apt′nes), ''n.'' The state or character of being unapt, in any sense.

'''unaquit†''', ''a.'' [ME., < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''aquit'', pp. of ''aquit'', ''v.''] Unrequited.
{{smaller block|Charitee goth ''unaquit''. {{right|''Gower'', Conf. Amant., ii.}}}}

'''unaraced†''', ''a.'' [ME., < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''araced''.] Same as ''unraced''.

'''unargued''' (un-är′gūd), ''a.'' '''1.''' Not argued; not debated; also, not argued with; not disputed; not opposed by argument.

