{{smaller block|My author and disposer, what thou bidd’st<br>''Unargued'' I obey.{{right|''Milton'', P. L., iv. 636.}}}}
'''2†.''' Not censured. ''B. Jonson.''

'''unarm''' (un-ärm′), ''v.'' [< ME. ''unarmen''; < ''un-''<sup>2</sup> + ''arm''<sup>2</sup>.] '''I.''' ''trans.'' '''1.''' To strip of armor or arms; disarm.
{{smaller block|To ''vnarme'' hym the kyng made in that place.{{right|''Rom. of Partenay'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 5666.}}
<poem>Sweet Helen, I must woo you
To help ''unarm'' our Hector.</poem>{{right|''Shak.'', T. and C., iii. 1. 163.}}}}
'''2.''' To render incapable of inflicting injury; make harmless.
{{smaller block|Galen would not leave the world too subtle a theory of poisons, ''unarming'' thereby the malice of venomous spirits.{{right|''Sir T. Browne.''}}}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To take off or lay aside one’s arms or armor.
{{smaller block|While thei were in ''vn-armynge'', thei saugh comynge the squyer of Elizer and the yoman.{{right|''Merlin'' (E. E. T. S.), iii. 555.}}
Will ye ''unarm'', and yield yourselves his prisoners?{{right|''Fletcher'', Humorous Lieutenant, iii. 7.}}}}

'''unarmed''' (un-ärmd′), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unarmed'', ''onarmed'', ''unarmit''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''armed''.] '''1.''' Not having on arms or armor; not equipped with arms or weapons.
{{smaller block|I am ''unarm’d''; forego this vantage, Greek.{{right|''Shak.'', T. and C., v. 8. 9.}}}}
